# The production profile (compose.prod.yaml, README "Production"): copy to .env.prod (ignored by Git), set the three
# secrets to random values of your own (for example: openssl rand -hex 32), and pass it to every command:
#   docker compose -f compose.prod.yaml --env-file .env.prod up -d --build --wait
# While a secret is empty, Compose refuses to start anything. The development stack's .env is never read.

PROD_APP_SECRET=
# The database owner's password. The database keeps the one it was created with: changing it once the database has
# data needs `docker compose -f compose.prod.yaml --env-file .env.prod down -v`, which deletes the data.
PROD_POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# The app's own role (app_runtime): the app container sets it again at each start, so it can change at any time.
PROD_APP_DATABASE_PASSWORD=

# Optional
# PROD_PORT=8490
# With a domain name, Caddy serves HTTPS itself (then publish ports 80 and 443: README, "Production").
# SERVER_NAME=scorewright.example.com
# The host names the API answers to (default: localhost and 127.0.0.1).
# TRUSTED_HOSTS=^scorewright\.example\.com$
# Behind a proxy that terminates TLS: its address, or private_ranges.
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=private_ranges
